u0027 guards. Align unit tests for all three layers.\u0022,\r\n      \u0022filePath\u0022: \u0022plugins/core/lib-sh/repl-daemon.js\u0022,\r\n      \u0022line\u0022: 49,\r\n      \u0022ruleId\u0022: \u0022TR-MCP-REPL-009-ENFORCE\u0022,\r\n      \u0022confidence\u0022: 0.95\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \u0022severity\u0022: \u0022medium\u0022,\r\n      \u0022category\u0022: \u0022consistency\u0022,\r\n      \u0022title\u0022: \u0022repl-invoke.ps1 and repl-bridge.ts share the same default-omit pattern as repl-daemon.js\u0022,\r\n      \u0022detail\u0022: \u0022Prior review flagged only the daemon. Current code still has the same conditional in PowerShell and TypeScript: psi.ArgumentList adds --agent only when AgentName -ne \u0027default\u0027 (repl-invoke.ps1:403-406); repl-bridge ensure() appends --agent only when agent \u0026\u0026 agent !== \u0027default\u0027 (repl-bridge.ts:86-87). Comments on those files claim every invocation must pass --agent, contradicting the guard.\u0022,\r\n      \u0022recommendation\u0022: \u0022Treat the three layers as one defect class. Unconditional --agent in all three, plus a small cross-layer assertion test (or pester/node unit) that spawn args always include --agent.\u0022,\r\n      \u0022filePath\u0022: \u0022plugins/core/lib-ps/repl-invoke.ps1\u0022,\r\n      \u0022line\u0022: 403,\r\n      \u0022ruleId\u0022: \u0022TR-MCP-REPL-009-ENFORCE-PS-TS\u0022,\r\n      \u0022confidence\u0022: 0.94\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \u0022severity\u0022: \u0022low\u0022,\r\n      \u0022category\u0022: \u0022testing\u0022,\r\n      \u0022title\u0022: \u0022Integration helper never passes --agent; named-agent E2E path is untested\u0022,\r\n      \u0022detail\u0022: \u0022ReplChildProcessHelper.cs adds --agent-stdio and optional --workspace-path/--marker-file but never --agent (around line 98). All integration child processes therefore use implicit GetCurrentAgent fallback (\u0027default\u0027 unless env is set). Per-agent VerifiedMarkerCacheEntry keying is only lightly touched by unit-style resolver tests that set AgentOverride one agent at a time; no integration test boots a non-default agent name end-to-end.\u0022,\r\n      \u0022recommendation\u0022: \u0022Add optional AgentName to ReplChildProcessHelper; pass --agent when set. Add at least one integration test with agent ClaudeCode or GrokCode validating resolution and cache file Agent field.\u0022,\r\n      \u0022filePath\u0022: \u0022tests/McpServer.Repl.IntegrationTests/ReplChildProcessHelper.cs\u0022,\r\n      \u0022line\u0022: 98,\r\n      \u0022ruleId\u0022: \u0022TEST-COVERAGE-AGENT-PARAM\u0022,\r\n      \u0022confidence\u0022: 0.92\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \u0022severity\u0022: \u0022low\u0022,\r\n      \u0022category\u0022: \u0022testing\u0022,\r\n      \u0022title\u0022: \u0022No test proves two agents keep separate verified-marker cache entries for one workspace\u0022,\r\n      \u0022detail\u0022: \u0022MarkerFileClientOptionsResolverTests sets AgentOverride to Codex, ClaudeCode, or Grok in separate tests but never resolves the same workspace under two agents and asserts two distinct cache rows. SaveVerifiedCacheEntry removes prior (workspace, agent) entries then WriteAllText without a file lock; concurrent multi-agent writers could interleave. No concurrency or dual-agent isolation test exists.\u0022,\r\n      \u0022recommendation\u0022: \u0022Add a unit test: resolve under Codex, then ClaudeCode, on one cache dir; deserialize verified-markers.json and assert two Agent values. Optionally add a file-mutex or atomic replace around SaveVerifiedCacheEntry and document concurrent-agent expectations.\u0022,\r\n      \u0022filePath\u0022: \u0022tests/McpServer.Repl.IntegrationTests/MarkerFileClientOptionsResolverTests.cs\u0022,\r\n      \u0022line\u0022: 412,\r\n      \u0022ruleId\u0022: \u0022TEST-DUAL-AGENT-CACHE-ISOLATION\u0022,\r\n      \u0022confidence\u0022: 0.88\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \u0022severity\u0022: \u0022low\u0022,\r\n      \u0022category\u0022: \u0022documentation\u0022,\r\n      \u0022title\u0022: \u0022AgentOverride XML docs say test-hook-only but production Program.cs sets it for CLI --agent\u0022,\r\n      \u0022detail\u0022: \u0022MarkerFileClientOptionsResolver.AgentOverride summary states \u0027Test hook only\u0027 (lines 32-36). Program.CreateHost sets AgentOverride from CLI --agent before resolution (Program.cs:83-86) and passes agentOverride into TryResolveWithDiagnostics. The property is a production propagation path, not only a test seam. Misleading docs increase risk of accidental removal or \u0027test-only\u0027 cleanup.\u0022,\r\n      \u0022recommendation\u0022: \u0022Update XML docs to describe AgentOverride as the production CLI/env override used for per-agent cache keying, with tests allowed to set it as a seam. Keep static early-set semantics or inject agent into resolver APIs without static mutable state if a later refactor is planned.\u0022,\r\n      \u0022filePath\u0022: \u0022src/McpServer.Repl.Host/MarkerFileClientOptionsResolver.cs\u0022,\r\n      \u0022line\u0022: 32,\r\n      \u0022ruleId\u0022: \u0022DOC-AGENTOVERRIDE-PRODUCTION\u0022,\r\n      \u0022confidence\u0022: 0.9\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \u0022severity\u0022: \u0022info\u0022,\r\n      \u0022category\u0022: \u0022architecture\u0022,\r\n      \u0022title\u0022: \u0022CLI --agent wiring and per-agent cache key model are sound\u0022,\r\n      \u0022detail\u0022: \u0022Program.cs defines --agent on both --agent-stdio and --interactive, sets AgentOverride early, and forwards agent into TryResolveWithDiagnostics. VerifiedMarkerCacheEntry is keyed by (WorkspacePath, Agent); TryUseCachedVerification and SaveVerifiedCacheEntry treat missing Agent as \u0027default\u0027 for backward compatibility; TTL is 24 hours. GetCurrentAgent falls back through MCP_AGENT_NAME, PLUGIN_AGENT_NAME, PLUGIN_AGENT_DEFAULT, then \u0027default\u0027. No regression observed in trust bootstrap structure or session-log SourceType